armacraft graphics patch 0003 -- UNAPPLIED. Opaque bike under shaderpacks, trail visible over water, no spikes at steps.

User report (2026-10-02, play-shaders, Complementary + Euphoria, client 0.1.6): "the bike is also translucent and on
water the line we leave behind is not visible as well as it is on land", plus red triangular spikes where the trail
crosses 1-block steps. Three causes, three fixes; only the first is specific to Iris.

1. Bike see-through under a shaderpack (Iris only)
   Cause: 0.1.6 assigns every pipeline to IrisProgram.EMISSIVE_ENTITIES (gbuffers_spidereyes). Iris gives that program
   a default blend override, SRC_ALPHA / ONE (ProgramId.SpiderEyes in Iris 1.11.7), which replaces the pipeline's own
   blend state. The opaque MODEL pipeline is therefore drawn additively: washed-out pink over bright ground, and
   whatever is behind the bike shows through it. (The same override is why trail walls are additive under a pack.)
   Fix:
   - IrisCompat assigns a program per pipeline: render.iris_model_program (bike, rim; default "entities" =
     gbuffers_entities, opaque and pack-lit; the vertices already carry a full-bright lightmap, so it stays bright at
     night), render.iris_wall_program (default "emissive": walls stay additive and glowing),
     render.iris_light_program (light pool, floor grid; default "emissive"). render.iris_program keeps "off" and "lit"
     (lit = walls through gbuffers_entities too, for packs with an empty spidereyes such as Alpha Piscium); any other
     value, including the "emissive" already written to existing config files, now means "use the per-pipeline keys".
   - ArmaRenderTypes: MODEL and SOLID_2S get the ALPHA_CUTOUT 0.1 define. Without it IrisApi.assignPipeline picks
     ShaderKey.ENTITIES_ALPHA for gbuffers_entities (the first key of that program), whose alpha test discards every
     fragment whose alpha is not above the vertex alpha: the bike was not drawn at all (seen in the first test run).
     With the define it picks ENTITIES_CUTOUT (alpha > 0.1). In vanilla the define discards alpha < 0.1; the cycle and
     rim textures are opaque.

2. Trail and light pool faint or invisible over water (general: any classic-transparency frame)
   Cause: SubmitNodeCollector.submitCustomGeometry puts blended render types into the "translucent custom geometry"
   phase, which LevelRenderer.executeClassicTransparency draws BEFORE translucent terrain. Walls and light pools do
   not write depth, so water, the frost walker's frosted ice (a translucent block) and glass are blended over them
   afterwards. Classic transparency runs whenever "improved transparency" is off (it is off in this user's
   options.txt) and always under Iris, which turns improved transparency off. Over a lake only the wall's top line
   (which writes depth) survived.
   Fix: new render/ArmaSubmit. Blended geometry (walls, light pool, floor grid, wall top lines) is submitted to the
   after-terrain phase (SubmitNodeCollection.afterTerrain, where vanilla draws translucent particles), so it blends
   over water like over land. With improved transparency on, all translucent phases are the same order-independent
   phase, so nothing changes there. render.walls_after_water=false restores the old phase (/armac reload applies it).

3. Spikes where the trail crosses 1-block steps (general: same with shaders off)
   Cause, two parts, both in ArmaTrailVisual:
   a. The join was put at the first block edge between the two trail points, or halfway when there was none. But the
      rider is a 0.6 wide box: Entity.move lifts it when its FRONT reaches the block and lets it down when its BACK has
      left it, so the recorded trail changes height up to 0.3 blocks before an up edge (measured: -0.068 for the edge
      at 0, 17.930 for the edge at 18). Half of all up steps had no edge between the two points and got a join half a
      block early: a piece of upper wall floating in front of the block face.
   b. The wall is exactly as tall as the step (trail_height 1.0, 1-block step), so the lower wall's top edge is the
      upper wall's bottom edge: the two levels touch along a line only. From the chase camera, behind and above, each
      step reads as two triangles meeting in a point (an hourglass).
   Fix:
   a. stepJoin(): the join goes to the block edge passed by the rider's front (up) or back (down) during that move,
      which may lie up to RIDER_HALF_WIDTH (0.3) outside the two points. An up join ahead of the newest point stays at
      that point until the trail is seen to continue past the edge (so a turn at that point does not overshoot); a
      down join behind the previous point pulls that point back to the edge.
   b. render.trail_step_ramp (default 1.0 block of run per block of rise, 0 = old vertical joins): on the low side of a
      step the wall top ramps up to the upper level, so the wall also covers the block face it climbs and both levels
      form one surface. The bottom still follows the ground; the wall texture stretches over the taller part.

Files: ArmacraftClient (init call, reload), ClientConfig (render.iris_model_program, iris_wall_program,
iris_light_program, walls_after_water, trail_step_ramp), render/IrisCompat, render/ArmaRenderTypes, render/ArmaSubmit
(new), render/ArenaRenderer and kit/BikeRenderer (submit through ArmaSubmit), ridekit/ArmaTrailVisual,
ArmaTrailVisualTest. No camera files.

